    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Latent Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) infection plays a significant role in the onset of lymphoproliferative disorders (LPDs), like post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder (PTLD).  EBV+ LPDs are characterized by uncontrolled proliferation of B lymphocytes driven, in part, by the viral oncogene latent membrane protein 1 (LMP1). Emerging evidence suggests natural killer (NK) cells, which help protect against malignancies and viruses, may play an important role in the immune response to EBV. We hypothesize that NK ligand expression by B cells, the host cell of EBV, may be regulated by EBV via LMP1 to circumvent immune responses during development of EBV-related LPDs. We compared expression of nine NK cell ligands in EBV-infected B cells to uninfected autologous B cells by flow cytometry.  We also investigated the role of LMP1 in regulating NK ligand expression using a signaling-inducible LMP1. We found, in accordance with published findings, that the NK activating ligand CD48 is upregulated by EBV infection. We also found EBV infection, in contrast to published findings, downregulates expression of the NK inhibitory ligands HLA-A,B,C and HLA-E. Furthermore, we show that LMP1 upregulates both HLA-A,B,C and HLA-E. These seemingly contradictory findings suggest a larger regulatory system involving other viral factors on the part of EBV that controls NK cell ligand expression and may be involved in coordinating immune evasion by EBV. Understanding such regulation may yield several potential therapeutic targets for treating EBV-related PTLDs.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
